  "text": "There is a hole in your vision right now, and your brain is painting over it. Your eye is a lens, an aperture and a sheet of sensor. Nothing exotic about it. The cornea does most of the bending. The lens does the fine focusing on top. The image lands on the back of your eye upside down, and it stays that way. But the cable has to leave somewhere, and where it leaves there are no sensors at all. That is a real blind spot in each eye, and you have never once seen it. Because your brain fills it with whatever is around it and hands you the finished result. And if the eyeball is slightly too long, the focus lands short of the back. That is short sight. Slightly too short and it lands behind. Glasses only move the focal point back onto the sensor. Your eye is decent optics with a very confident editor sitting behind it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
